  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1050 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nA fairly calm overnight period is progressing across the Mid-\nSouth currently with temperatures spanning the 70s. Earlier \nshower and thunderstorm activity has continued to wane and move \nout of the area, leaving behind dry conditions. With generally \nlight winds and low dew point depressions overnight, expect for \npatchy fog to develop over the next few hours through sunrise, \nespecially for locations that saw precipitation Saturday \nafternoon. Any fog that does develop will quickly mix out \nfollowing sunrise.\n\nBeginning on Sunday, we will slowly transition back into a true,\nsummertime pattern which will likely continue for the foreseeable\nfuture. With generally little upper-level forcing, the majority\nof any shower and thunderstorm activity will remain diurnally\ndriven. Coverage may be a bit more scattered on Sunday, mainly\nsouth of I-40, but coverage will likely remain isolated by Monday\nthrough at least midweek. Temperatures will remain closer to\nnormal on Sunday with lingering cloud coverage, generally in the\nupper 80s to low 90s. By Monday and Tuesday with clearing\nconditions, high temperatures will quickly rise back to the low\nto mid 90s. Probabilistic guidance is still fairly unimpressed\nwith the potential for heat index values exceeding 105 degrees on\nSunday, but probabilities begin to increase to around 20 to 30%\nby Monday and even higher Tuesday onward. As such, we will\ncontinue to monitor the potential for heat headlines as the week\nprogresses as portions of the Mid-South will likely meet or\nexceed the criteria for issuance.\n\nBy Wednesday into Thursday, an upper-level ridge will begin to\nstrengthen over the Rockies with a low pressure system moving\nthrough the Great Lakes into the northeast. While the deepening\ntrough over the midwest will allow our area to shift to subtle\nnorthwesterly flow, latest guidance trends quickly rebuild a high\npressure over our area into next weekend. With the potential\nnorthwest flow regime, this would open the door for a few\nshortwaves to swing down through the region and increase PoP\nchances. However, guidance is trending a little warmer and drier,\nleaning more towards a weaker northwesterly flow pattern over the\nMid-South. Latest LREF guidance does tend to favor a drier, more\nsummertime pattern through the remainder of the week. ENS\nguidance leans more towards a gradual warming trend through the\nweek and into next weekend, while the GEFS starts much warmer and\nfalls back closer to normal for our temperatures by the end of\nthe week. Nonetheless, summer is in full swing for the Mid-South\nwith highs likely remaining in the low to mid 90s to end the\nweek.",
